<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Semnones</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Semnones were a Germanic people living between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the Oder in the 1st and 2nd centuries CE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> called them the oldest and noblest of the Suebi and described the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/human-sacrifice">human sacrifices</a> held in their sacred grove. They are thought to have formed the core of the later Alamanni and Juthungi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/semnones.jpg" alt="Semnones" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Lancaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suebi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
